course: biology
lesson: 334
title: The garden in summer: flowers, aphids, ladybirds and flying ant day
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A summer garden is a busy little town of deals and hunts: some animals farm, some hunt, and some just grow faster than anyone can eat them.
explain: Look at a rose stem covered in tiny green aphids sucking sweet sap. In summer each mother gives birth to live babies without needing a father, and each baby already has its own baby growing inside, so a few aphids become thousands in days.
explain: Then come the hunters: a seven-spot ladybird and its spiky grey larva munch through them, while ants walk up the stem to stroke the aphids and drink their sweet honeydew, guarding them from ladybirds in return.
explain: Over it all, on a warm July afternoon, the air fills with flying ants, and swifts scream over the rooftops catching them; these birds eat, drink and even sleep on the wing.
fact: Aphids suck sugary sap from plant stems, and in summer female aphids give birth to live babies without a father, so their numbers explode.
fact: A baby aphid is born with its own baby already growing inside it.
fact: Both adult ladybirds and their spiky larvae eat aphids, and one ladybird is reported to eat thousands of aphids in its life, so gardeners welcome them as free pest control.
fact: The seven-spot ladybird is named for its spots; the number of spots does not show its age, and its bright colours warn birds that it tastes bad.
fact: Aphids give out a sweet drop called honeydew, ants drink it, and in return ants guard the aphids from ladybirds, which people call «milking» the aphids.
fact: On warm, damp days winged young queens and males leave many ant nests at once; gulls and swifts feast on them, and these flights happen over several weeks, not just one day.
fact: Longer, unmown grass lets clover, daisies and other flowers bloom and feed bees and butterflies, and «No Mow May» asks people to skip mowing for a month.
fact: Grasshoppers chirp by rubbing their back legs against their wings, crickets rub their wings together, and the warmer it is, the faster they chirp.
fact: Cuckoo spit is froth made by a young froghopper insect to hide from enemies and stay damp; it has nothing to do with cuckoos and is named because it appears when cuckoos call.
fact: Swifts spend almost their whole lives flying, even sleeping on the wing; they come to Britain in May and leave in August, and a young swift may fly for about two years without landing.
fact: Birds bathe to keep their feathers clean and working well; fresh water helps them in hot, dry weather, and starlings bathe in noisy crowds.
exercise: What do aphids feed on? | Sugary sap sucked from plant stems | Pollen from the flowers only, never the leaves | Other insects | Soil and stones
exercise: How can aphids cover a plant in just a few days? | In summer mothers give birth to live babies without a father | Each aphid lays a million tiny eggs at once on the underside of a leaf | They fly in from far away every night | They split in two like a worm
exercise: What is strange about a newborn aphid? | It already has its own baby growing inside it | It is born with wings and flies off at once to a new plant | It is bigger than its mother | It has no legs for a week
exercise: Which hunt aphids on a rose? | Both adult ladybirds and their spiky larvae | Only adult ladybirds, never the spiky larvae | Only ants | Only the aphids' own mothers
exercise: Why do gardeners welcome ladybirds? | They eat aphids for free | They make roses smell sweeter | They spin webs to catch flies | They water the plants with dew
exercise: What do the spots on a seven-spot ladybird tell you? | Which kind of ladybird it is | How many years old the ladybird is | How many babies it has had so far | How many aphids it has eaten today
exercise: Why are ladybirds brightly coloured? | To warn birds that they taste bad | To attract aphids to come closer to be eaten | To hide among red flowers | To keep warm in the sun
exercise: Why do ants stroke aphids? | To get the sweet honeydew the aphids give out | To keep them from biting the plant and killing it | To carry them off as food | To clean them for the ladybirds
exercise: What do aphids get from the ants? | Protection from ladybirds | Water carried from the ants' nest | A ride to a new plant every day | Food the ants bring from the kitchen
exercise: Which ants fly out of the nest on a warm, damp summer day? | Winged young queens and males | All the worker ants, leaving the nest empty | Only the old queen | Only the baby ants
exercise: Is «flying ant day» really one day? | No, flights happen across several weeks | Yes, always the first of July | Yes, the last day of summer | No, it happens only in winter
exercise: What is «No Mow May»? | A campaign asking people to skip mowing for a month | A competition in May to see who can keep the shortest grass | A law against lawnmowers | A day when bees mow the lawn
exercise: How does a grasshopper chirp? | By rubbing its back legs against its wings | By blowing air out hard through little holes in its nose | By rubbing its wings together | By clicking its jaws
exercise: What makes cuckoo spit on a plant stem? | A young froghopper insect | A cuckoo spitting from a tree | A snail's sticky slime trail | Rain mixed with yellow pollen
exercise: Which bird may fly for about two years without landing? | A young swift | A young blackbird | A young wood pigeon | A young mallard duck
exercise: Why do birds splash in a bird bath? | To keep their feathers clean and working well | To wash off their bright colours before the winter | To catch fish | To lay eggs in the water
test: «ladybird's got 7 spots so it's 7 years old innit» What is right? | No, the spots show its kind, not its age | No, it is 14 years old, two years for each spot | Yes, one new spot grows every single year | Yes, and it loses one of its spots each winter
test: «gross, the rose is covered in green bugs, just spray the lot» What could a gardener do instead? | Leave the ladybirds to eat the aphids | Pull up the whole rose bush and plant a new one | Spray the ladybirds too | Pour milk on the rose
test: What would happen to the aphids if all the ladybirds in a garden disappeared? | Their numbers would grow even faster | They would all die out without ladybirds | They would slowly turn into ladybirds | Nothing would change in the garden at all
test: «the ants are milking the aphids like cows, lol that's not real» What is right? | It is real: ants drink their honeydew and guard them | Not real, ants only ever eat aphids and never look after them | Aphids milk the ants instead | Ants and aphids never meet
test: Why would ants from many nests all fly at the same time? | So young queens and males from different nests can meet | Because the nests are flooded | Because they are chasing swifts | Because one ant tells all the others by phone
test: «mum won't mow the lawn, says it's for the bees, she's just lazy» What is true? | Longer grass lets clover and daisies flower, which feed bees | Bees eat the grass itself, so long grass is simply more food for them | Bees prefer short grass | Long grass scares bees away
test: «grasshoppers going mad today, it's boiling» Why would they chirp faster? | Grasshoppers chirp faster when it is warmer | They chirp faster when it is cold | Heat makes their legs longer | They are shouting for water
test: In a cartoon a cuckoo spits froth onto the plants. What really makes cuckoo spit? | A young froghopper hiding in froth it makes itself | A real cuckoo, just as in the cartoon | A frog that lives on the plant | Raindrops beaten by the wind
test: How could a swift sleep while flying? | It sleeps in short naps high in the air while gliding | It lands on a soft cloud and sleeps there for a few hours | It never sleeps at all | It sleeps standing on a roof
test: Count them: 3 grasshoppers hop onto the path and 1 ladybird lands on a leaf. How many legs are there? | 24 | 18 | 16 | 32
test: «no point leaving water out for birds, they just get it from puddles» What is right on a hot, dry day? | Fresh water helps birds drink and bathe when puddles dry up | Birds never drink water at all, they get all they need from their food | Birds only bathe in the sea | Water makes birds too heavy to fly
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the population of aphids upon a plant may increase greatly within a few days during the summer. | Female aphids produce live young without the participation of a father, each young already bearing young of its own | Aphids migrate to the plant in great numbers from distant regions each night | Each aphid deposits several million eggs upon a single occasion | Aphids divide into two separate individuals at intervals of one minute
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the relationship between ants and aphids. | Ants consume the honeydew secreted by aphids and in return protect them from ladybirds | Aphids supply ants with water in exchange for shelter within the nest during the winter months | Ants consume aphids exclusively as prey | The two insects avoid all contact with one another
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the spots upon a seven-spot ladybird? | They indicate the species of the ladybird and bear no relation to its age | They correspond exactly to the age of the insect in years | They increase in number with each aphid consumed | They are acquired from the flowers upon which it rests
exam: Ascertain the origin of the substance commonly designated «cuckoo spit». | It is a froth produced by the young of the froghopper for concealment and moisture | It is the saliva of the cuckoo deposited upon plants | It is a secretion of the plant in response to heat | It is a residue left upon stems by passing snails
